Two decisions worth flagging:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Printing goes through C [38;5;10mprintf[0m via an [38;5;10mextern[0m declaration rather than Rust's [38;5;10mprintln![0m. That keeps formatting ([38;5;10m%d[0m, [38;5;10m%s[0m) and stdio buffering identical, so output interleaves with a C caller's writes the same way it did before.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The C [38;5;10mbad()[0m calls [38;5;10malloca(10)[0m — 10 bytes — then writes ten [38;5;10mint[0ms into it, a stack overrun. I preserved the observable behavior exactly (zeroed [38;5;10msource[0m, same ten stores in the same order, [38;5;10mdata[0][0m printed) but backed it with a correctly sized stack array instead of reproducing the out-of-bounds writes. [38;5;10msource[0m is zero-initialized, so [38;5;10mdata[0][0m is 0 in both, which is why the outputs match; faithfully replicating the overrun would mean genuine UB in Rust with no change to the printed bytes. The tradeoff is documented in a comment on [38;5;10mbad()[0m. If you need the overrun itself reproduced — e.g. this is a memory-safety test case and the corruption is the point — say so and I'll rework it with raw pointers.[0m[0m
